---
import { externalServices, privacyContact, privacyPolicyEffectiveDate } from "../../data/privacy.ts";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const contactUrl = "mailto:" + privacyContact;
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description="How we handle data about your visits to Open Paleo: what we count, which services handle your requests, and your rights.">
    <article class="privacy-page">
        <header class="privacy-header">
            <h1>Privacy</h1>
            <p class="privacy-summary">
                We count visits to Open Paleo so we can see how the site is used. We don’t set cookies, and we don’t
                keep anything that identifies you.
            </p>
            <p class="privacy-effective">Effective {privacyPolicyEffectiveDate}</p>
        </header>

        <section>
            <h2>Who runs this site</h2>
            <p>
                Open Paleo is a volunteer project. The project’s maintainers run this site and are responsible for this
                policy. If you have a question or request about privacy, email us at
                <a href={contactUrl}>{privacyContact}</a>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>What we collect, and why</h2>
            <p>
                We count visits to learn which pages people read, which browsers and devices we need to support, and
                how people find the site. Plausible Analytics does the counting for us (see
                <a href="#services">services</a> below). For each page you open, Plausible receives the data described in
                <a href="https://plausible.io/data-policy">Plausible’s data policy</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Other sites can show an Open Paleo infobox, a short summary of a genus, on their own pages. An infobox
                is a page of open-paleo.org, so we handle it like any other page: GitHub Pages serves it, and Plausible
                counts it as a visit, recording the site that showed it as where the visit came from.
            </p>
            <p>
                All we see are totals: how many people visited, which pages they read, which browsers and devices they
                used, and where the visits came from. None of it identifies you.
            </p>
            <p>
                Where data protection law applies to this counting, such as the European Union’s General Data
                Protection Regulation (GDPR), our legal basis is our legitimate interest in understanding how the site
                is used. The counting is designed so that no visitor can be identified.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>What we don’t collect</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    There are no accounts or sign-ins, and nothing you type on the site is sent to us. When you search,
                    your search terms are part of the page’s address, which GitHub Pages receives with the request;
                    Plausible discards them.
                </li>
                <li>
                    We don’t set cookies or store anything about you in your browser. The one thing the site does keep
                    there is the tree extractor’s text-recognition data, so the tool starts faster next time. It contains
                    nothing about you, and the figures you open in either tool are never uploaded.
                </li>
                <li>There’s no advertising, and nothing on the site tracks you across other sites.</li>
                <li>We don’t sell or share data about visitors.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 id="services">Services that handle your requests</h2>
            <p>
                These services receive your requests, either because they host the site or because a page asks your
                browser to contact them. To find out what each one collects and what it does with it, see its privacy
                or data policy.
            </p>
            {externalServices.map((service) => (
                <section class="service">
                    <h3>{service.name}</h3>
                    <dl>
                        <dt>Operator</dt>
                        <dd>{service.operator}</dd>
                        <dt>What it does</dt>
                        <dd>{service.role}</dd>
                        <dt>When your browser contacts it</dt>
                        <dd>{service.whenContacted}</dd>
                        <dt>What it receives</dt>
                        <dd>{service.receives}</dd>
                        <dt>Its policy</dt>
                        <dd><a href={service.policyUrl}>{service.policyName}</a></dd>
                    </dl>
                </section>
            ))}
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>How long data is kept</h2>
            <p>
                We keep only the visit totals in Plausible, for as long as the site uses Plausible. When we close our
                Plausible account, they’re deleted with it. For how long each service keeps data, see its policy above.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Your rights</h2>
            <p>
                Depending on where you live, you may have the right to see, correct or delete personal data about you,
                or to object to its use. We don’t hold anything that identifies you, so there’s usually nothing for us
                to find, but email <a href={contactUrl}>{privacyContact}</a> and we’ll help, including with requests to
                the services above. You can also block the visit counting with your browser’s privacy settings or an
                extension, and the site will work just the same. If you’re in the European Union or the United Kingdom,
                you have the right to complain to a data protection authority, such as the one in the country where you
                live or work.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Children</h2>
            <p>
                The site is for a general audience. Nothing we collect identifies anyone, including children.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
            <p>
                “Suggest a correction” and “Report an error” open a form on GitHub, where you sign in under GitHub’s
                terms. References link to their publishers, and the header and footer link to our GitHub, Bluesky and
                Threads pages. Once you follow a link off Open Paleo, that site’s own policy applies.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
            <p>
                If we change how the site handles data, we’ll update this page and its effective date. You’ll find every
                earlier version in
                <a href="https://github.com/open-paleo/web/commits/main/src/data/privacy.ts">the site’s history on GitHub</a>.
            </p>
        </section>
    </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .privacy-page
    {
        display: grid;
        gap: var(--space-6);
        max-width: var(--measure);
    }

    .privacy-header
    {
        display: grid;
        gap: var(--space-3);
    }

    h1
    {
        font-size: var(--text-display);
        font-weight: 800;
    }

    .privacy-summary
    {
        color: var(--color-muted);
    }

    .privacy-effective
    {
        color: var(--color-muted);
        font-family: var(--font-sans);
        font-size: var(--text-small);
    }

    section
    {
        display: grid;
        gap: var(--space-3);
    }

    ul
    {
        display: grid;
        gap: var(--space-2);
        margin: 0;
    }

    .service
    {
        gap: var(--space-2);
        padding-top: var(--space-3);
        border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    }

    .service dl
    {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 9rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
        gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
        margin: 0;
    }

    .service dt
    {
        color: var(--color-muted);
        font-family: var(--font-sans);
        font-size: var(--text-small);
    }

    .service dd
    {
        margin: 0;
    }

    @media (max-width: 30rem)
    {
        .service dl
        {
            grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
            gap: var(--space-1);
        }

        .service dd + dt
        {
            margin-top: var(--space-2);
        }
    }
</style>
